   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to establish a juvenile justice education task force to address\neffective coordination and programmatic coherence in the provision of\neducational opportunities and services to certain students; and providing for\nthe repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S9336",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S9336-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "",
    "lawSection": "Education",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S9336\n\nSPONSOR: BAILEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to establish a juvenile justice education task force to address\neffective coordination and programmatic coherence in the provision of\neducational opportunities and services to certain students; and provid-\ning for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nThe purpose of the bill is to establish a task force to study and devel-\nop recommendations for the improvement of educational programming across\nthe spectrum of juvenile justice confinement settings and the educa-\ntional transition of youth post-confinement.  The task force will report\ntheir recommendations to the Board of Regents, the Governor, and the\nlegislature by December 31, 2022.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill sets forth legislative findings and declarations.\n\nSection 2 of the bill establishes a task force on juvenile justice\neducation to study and develop recommendations for the improvement of\neducational programming across the spectrum of juvenile justice confine-\nment settings and the educational transition of youth post-confinement.\nThe bill requires the task force to be comprised of 14 members, which\nshall include the commissioner of education or his or her designee, who\nshall serve as the chair of the task force, four members appointed by\nthe Commissioner of Education, five members appointed by the Governor,\ntwo members appointed upon recommendation of the Temporary President of\nthe Senate, and two members appointed upon recommendation of the Speaker\nof the Assembly.\n\nSection 3 of the bill directs the task force to study and provide recom-\nmendations on: (1) the provision of educational opportunities and\nservices to students in juvenile justice settings; (2) the creation of a\nstatewide entity to provide or oversee such educational opportunities\nand services; (3) oversight of the provision of educational services by\nthe education department and the office of children and family services;\n(4) access to federal and state funding mechanisms; (5) access to\ncareer, occupational, certificate, licensure and technical courses; (6)\naccess to literacy and English language learner services; (7) access to\nspecial education services; (8) access to programs leading to a high\nschool diploma or a high school equivalency diploma; (9) access to make\nup credit programs; (10) access to programs and services through the\neducation department's office of adult career and continuing education\nservices - vocational rehabilitation and board of cooperative educa-\ntional services; (10) access to student records; (11) appropriate\naccountability measures; and (12) educational transition services\nprovided to students returning to the community after placement or\nconfinement. Additionally, such section requires the task force to\nsubmit a final report of its recommendations, including any legislative\nrecommendations, to the Board of Regents, the governor, the Temporary\nPresident of the Senate, the Speaker of the Assembly and the Chairs of\neducation committees in the Senate and Assembly by December 31, 2022.\n\n\nSection 4 of the bill provides that, subject to appropriation, the task\nforce may contract with consultants, including institutions of higher\neducation and independent research organizations, to assist the task\nforce in the performance of its duties.\n\nSection 5 is the effective date.\n\n \nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT OF THE BILL:\n\nResearch has documented that a significant number of the adolescents who\nare alleged to or have been found to have committed crimes and enter the\njuvenile justice system are significantly over age and under credited,\nsome reading only at the elementary age level. Most youths enter\ndetention and placement facilities with a range of intense educational,\nmental health, and social needs. Many are already disengaged from\nschool, having dropped out or been chronically absent. Additional barri-\ners to educational services provided in in these settings can include\nchallenges to physical space, insufficient funding, inadequate time for\nclasses, and the lack of incorporation of research-based instructional\nand behavioral approaches.\n\nAt the present time, there is a significant lack of effective coordi-\nnation and programmatic coherence in the provision of educational oppor-\ntunities and services to students in juvenile justice settings. When\nyouth must be confined, detention and placement settings provide an\nopportunity to re-engage them in their education and future and to reset\ntheir trajectory. These programs must plan for and support youth reentry\nto the community, including working with the student's school of reentry\nto ensure credits are granted for work accomplished while in confinement\nand re-enrollment in school in the community.\n\nEducational programs provided in New York's juvenile justice settings\nhave struggled to recruit and retain New York State certified teachers;\nprovide access to all core academic courses required to obtain a New\nYork State Regents Diploma; and design quality, culturally and linguis-\ntically responsive and engaging instruction to meet the extensive, crit-\nical needs of this consistently shifting student population. Lack of\naccess to state and federal education funds for core educational\nservices, delayed access to students' records, rolling admissions and\ndischarges, as well as barriers to re-enrollment, and providing supports\nto students as they transition back to public and nonpublic schools have\ncontributed to this situation.\n\nIncarcerated youth funds were originally intended to support students\nbetween the ages of 16 and 21 in county jails. Since implementation of\nNew York's \"raise the age law\" pursuant to part WWW of chapter 59 of the\nlaws of 2017, incarcerated youth funds for jails have declined, as 16-\nand 17-year-old students are placed in detention and other settings\nwhich are ineligible for incarcerated youth funding.  This makes it\ndifficult to sustain staffing and educational programming for incarcer-\nated 18- to 21-year-old students, who are still entitled to an educa-\ntion.\n\nResearch makes clear that engaging youth in the juvenile justice system\nin their education and future is key to reducing recidivism.  Youth who\nachieve higher levels of educational achievement while confined are more\nlikely to return to school and earn a diploma after release.\n\nIn light of the above, this bill is necessary to establish a temporary\ntask force to review and study the needs of educational programming\nacross the spectrum of juvenile justice confinement settings as well as\nthe educational transition of youth post-confinement. The task force\nwill then identify actional recommendations for the improvement of the\neducation provided to youth in juvenile justice settings and make such\nrecommendations to the Board of Regents, the Governor, and the Legisla-\nture by December 31, 2022.\n\n \nBUDGETARY IMPLICATIONS OF THE BILL:\n\nThe bill requires that the task force members receive their actual and\nnecessary expenses incurred in the performance of their duties, provided\nthat the task force shall be authorized to conduct meetings in a manner\nthat minimizes travel and costs as determined by the Commissioner. Addi-\ntionally, the bill provides that, subject to appropriation, the task\nforce may contract with consultants, including institutions of higher\neducation and independent research organizations, to assist the task\nforce in the performance of its duties and the State Education Depart-\nment has requested one additional staff support person to implement the\nbill.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall expire and be deemed\nrepealed on the sixtieth day after the task force on juvenile justice\neducation shall have submitted the report required by section three of\nthis act; provided that the commissioner of education shall notify the\nlegislative bill drafting commission upon filing the report required in\nsection three of this act in order that the commission may maintain an\naccurate and timely effective data base of the official text of the laws\nof the state of New York in furtherance of effectuating the provisions\nof section 44 of the legislative law and section 70-b of the public\nofficers law.\n",
